  "configuration_id": "config_021",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_010 (pca_n2000, resnet50, multiprofile, avgpool, concat) is the strongest result so far at 0.6186 acc, and it clearly outperforms its singleprofile counterpart config_009 (0.5266), establishing multiprofile+avgpool as a key design choice. We also know pca_n2000 > scgpt for RNA (config_009 vs config_004). However, no experiment has yet tested a stronger image encoder (imagenet_vit_l) in the multiprofile setting. Question: Does upgrading the image encoder from resnet50 to imagenet_vit_l improve performance when all other components (pca_n2000, multiprofile, avgpool, concat) are held at their proven best? Why this is the best next experiment: config_021 changes exactly one variable (image encoder) relative to the current best config_010, cleanly isolating the effect of image representation quality. If imagenet_vit_l helps, it identifies image encoding as a further lever; if it does not, it tells us the bottleneck lies elsewhere (aggregation, fusion, RNA encoder) and we can redirect the remaining budget accordingly. This is the highest-information single-variable test available."
}

